      "math_academy_id": "q-16419",
      "knowledge_point_id": "37a4168b-1425-5f3a-a577-4b5884906a52",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Sum of a Complex Number and Its Conjugate",
      "problem": "If $z=5+5\\text{i}$, calculate $z+\\overset{―}{z}.$",
      "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\nWhen we add a complex number and its conjugate, the result is always twice the real part of the complex number.\n\nFor the given complex number $z=5+5\\text{i},$ the real part is $Re(z)=5,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\overset{―}{z} & =2\\text{Re}(z) \\\\  & =2(5) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAlternatively, we can actually carry out the process of adding the complex number and its conjugate.\n\nTo obtain the conjugate of the complex number $z=5+5\\text{i},$ we flip the sign of the imaginary part and get $\\overset{―}{z}=5-5\\text{i}.$\n\nAdding, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\overset{―}{z} & =(5+5\\text{i})+(5-5\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(5+5)+\\text{i}(5-5) \\\\  & =10+\\text{i}0 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
