      "problem": "If $z=4+7\\text{i}$, calculate $z+\\overset{―}{z}.$",
      "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\nWhen we add a complex number and its conjugate, the result is always twice the real part of the complex number.\n\nFor the given complex number $z=4+7\\text{i},$ the real part is $Re(z)=4,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\overset{―}{z} & =2\\text{Re}(z) \\\\  & =2(4) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAlternatively, we can actually carry out the process of adding the complex number and its conjugate.\n\nTo obtain the conjugate of the complex number $z=4+7\\text{i},$ we flip the sign of the imaginary part and get $\\overset{―}{z}=4-7\\text{i}.$\n\nAdding, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\overset{―}{z} & =(4+7\\text{i})+(4-7\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(4+4)+\\text{i}(7-7) \\\\  & =8+\\text{i}0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
