      "math_academy_id": "q-159758",
      "knowledge_point_id": "64cd3ef1-6d41-50c6-b09d-cb709d89281f",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Product of a Complex Number and Its Conjugate",
      "problem": "Given that $z=9+3\\text{i},$ calculate $z\\overset{―}{z}.$",
      "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\nWhen we multiply a complex number and its conjugate, the result is always the sum of squares of the real part and the imaginary part.\n\nFor the given complex number $z=9+3\\text{i},$ the real part is $\\text{Re}(z)=9,$ and the imaginary part is $\\text{Im}(z)=3,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\overset{―}{z} & =(\\text{Re}(z))^{2}+(\\text{Im}(z))^{2} \\\\  & ={9}^{2}+{3}^{2} \\\\  & =81+9 \\\\  & =90.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAlternatively, we can actually carry out the process of multiplying the complex number and its conjugate.\n\nTo obtain the conjugate of the complex number $z=9+3\\text{i},$ we flip the sign of the imaginary part and get $\\overset{―}{z}=9-3\\text{i}.$\n\nMultiplying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\overset{―}{z} & =(9+3\\text{i})(9-3\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={9}^{2}+(9)(3)\\text{i}+(-3)(9)\\text{i}-{\\text{i}}^{2}(3)^{2} \\\\  & =81+27\\text{i}-27\\text{i}-(-1)(9) \\\\  & =81+9 \\\\  & =90.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
